1052:
Single electron species obey the Bohr energy E_{n} = 2.18 \times 10^{-18} \times Z^{2} / n^{2} J atom^{-1}
For \text{Li}^{2+} the nuclear charge is Z = 3 and the remaining electron is in n = 1
Ionisation energy is therefore E = 2.18 \times 10^{-18} \times 9 / 1 = 19.62 \times 10^{-18} J atom^{-1}
Rewriting gives E = 1.962 \times 10^{-17} J atom^{-1} → (C)

1053:
Molecular orbital bond orders are 2.5 for \text{O}_{2}^{+}, 2.0 for \text{O}_{2}, 1.5 for \text{O}_{2}^{-} and 1.0 for \text{O}_{2}^{2-}
Bond length shortens as bond order rises, so length grows in the order \text{O}_{2}^{+} < \text{O}_{2} < \text{O}_{2}^{-} < \text{O}_{2}^{2-} and the first statement holds
Unpaired electron counts are 2 in \text{O}_{2}, 1 in \text{O}_{2}^{+}, 1 in \text{O}_{2}^{-} and 0 in \text{O}_{2}^{2-}
Since the two singly charged ions tie at 1, the claimed strict fall fails and the second statement is false
First statement true with second false → (C)

1056:
Silver chromate dissociates as \text{Ag}_{2}\text{CrO}_{4} \rightleftharpoons 2\text{Ag}^{+} + \text{CrO}_{4}^{2-} with K_{sp} = 4s^{3}
Hence s_{1}^{3} = 32x / 4 = 8x and s_{1} = 2x^{1/3}
Silver bromide dissociates as \text{AgBr} \rightleftharpoons \text{Ag}^{+} + \text{Br}^{-} with K_{sp} = s^{2}
Hence s_{2}^{2} = 4y and s_{2} = 2y^{1/2}
Molarity ratio is s_{1} / s_{2} = x^{1/3} / y^{1/2} → (D)

1061:
Acidified dichromate stays orange unless a reductant converts chromium(VI) to green \text{Cr}^{3+}
First set holds \text{Fe}^{2+}, \text{Sn}^{2+}, \text{I}^{-} and \text{S}^{2-}, every member capable of donating electrons
Remaining options each hide an already oxidised ion such as \text{Fe}^{3+}, \text{Sn}^{4+} or \text{SO}_{4}^{2-}
Such oxidised ions cannot reduce chromium(VI), so only the first set reacts completely
Only the first listed set discharges the colour in every case → (A)

1062:
Spectrochemical strength rises as \text{F}^{-} < \text{H}_{2}\text{O} < en < \text{CN}^{-}
Splitting therefore grows in the same order across the four chromium(III) complexes
Largest splitting 26600 cm^{-1} belongs to [\text{Cr}(\text{CN})_{6}]^{3-} and smallest 15060 cm^{-1} to [\text{CrF}_{6}]^{3-}
Middle values place 17400 cm^{-1} on the aqua complex and 22300 cm^{-1} on the en complex
Pairing is A-IV, B-I, C-II and D-III, the fourth listed match → (D)

